---
import ConverterPage from '../components/ConverterPage.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'What counts as "Mangal" text for this converter?',
    a: 'Any Unicode Hindi. Mangal is a font rather than an encoding, so text displayed in Mangal, Nirmala UI, Noto Sans Devanagari, Aparajita or Kokila is all the same underlying Unicode. Paste from a Word file, a website, a PDF or WhatsApp. It all works.',
  },
  {
    q: 'The output is English letters, not Hindi. What went wrong?',
    a: 'Nothing. Kruti Dev text <em>is</em> ASCII, and it only becomes Hindi once the Kruti Dev font is applied. Use the Download .docx button, which sets the font for you, or paste into Word and choose <strong>Kruti Dev 010</strong> from the font list.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why would anyone convert Unicode back to a legacy font?',
    a: 'Typing tests and stenography exams in several states are still conducted in Kruti Dev, so candidates need practice passages in it. Printing presses and DTP operators often have decades of Kruti Dev templates. And offices regularly receive a Unicode draft that has to be dropped into an old legacy-font layout.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Will my numbers and dates survive?',
    a: 'Yes. Numbers, dates, percentages, URLs and email addresses are left exactly as they are by default. That matters here, because converting the digits in an account number or an order date would corrupt it silently. The checkbox under the panels turns this off if you actually want Devanagari numerals.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does it handle conjuncts and the reph?',
    a: 'Yes. The i-matra is moved back to the front of its cluster, the reph is moved to the end of its syllable, composed vowel signs are split back into the two glyphs Kruti Dev draws them with, and क्ष, त्र, ज्ञ, श्र plus the nukta letters are all mapped.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need Mangal installed?',
    a: 'No. You need a Devanagari font to <em>read</em> the input panel, and almost every modern device has one. You do not need Mangal specifically, and you do not need it installed for the conversion itself.',
  },
];
---

<ConverterPage
  path="/mangal-to-kruti-dev"
  lede="Paste Mangal or any Unicode Hindi on the left and get Kruti Dev 010 on the right. For typing practice, legacy templates and old DTP files."
  faq={faq}
>
  <h2>Mangal to Kruti Dev in one step</h2>
  <p>
    Mangal is a Unicode font, so "Mangal text" is just Unicode Hindi: the same
    text you would get from a website, a modern Word document or a WhatsApp
    message. Kruti Dev is a legacy font that stores Hindi as ASCII in
    <em>visual</em> order. Going from one to the other means undoing every
    reordering rule that Unicode applies.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is what this page does as you type: short-i signs move back in front of
    their consonants, rephs move to the end of their syllables, composed vowel
    signs like <strong lang="hi">ो</strong> split back into their two drawn
    parts, and half letters are re-encoded to the separate glyphs Kruti Dev uses
    for them.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading the output</h2>
  <p>
    The right-hand panel will look like nonsense English, something along the
    lines of <code>Hkkjr</code>. That is correct and expected. Kruti Dev text
    only becomes Hindi when the Kruti Dev font is applied to it. Two ways to get
    there:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Download .docx</strong>. The Word file comes out with Kruti Dev
      already applied to every paragraph.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Copy text</strong>, paste into your document, select it, and set
      the font to <strong>Kruti Dev 010</strong>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If it still shows English letters after setting the font, the font is not
    installed on that machine. See
    <a href="/kruti-dev-font-download">Kruti Dev font download</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Typing tests and legacy templates</h2>
  <p>
    The most common reason people need this direction is exam practice. CPCT in
    Madhya Pradesh, RSCIT in Rajasthan and several state stenography and
    junior-assistant tests are still conducted in Kruti Dev, and practice
    passages are almost always published as Unicode. Converting them here gives
    you material in the font the exam actually uses.
  </p>
  <p>
    The other common reason is production work. A press with twenty years of
    Kruti Dev layouts cannot re-set them all in Unicode, so incoming Unicode
    copy has to be converted to fit the existing template.
  </p>

  <h2>Round-tripping</h2>
  <p>
    Kruti Dev frequently offers more than one key sequence for the same letter,
    so the output here uses one canonical spelling rather than reproducing
    exactly what a human typist would have pressed. It renders identically, and
    running it back through the
    <a href="/">Kruti Dev to Unicode converter</a> returns the text you started
    with. If you want the forward direction with Mangal framing, use
    <a href="/kruti-dev-to-mangal">Kruti Dev to Mangal</a>.
  </p>
</ConverterPage>
